WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.180
- [Narrator] Puppies,
they're the furry friends

00:00:02.180 --> 00:00:04.100
we take for granted.

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:06.595
But, how does having one affect us?

00:00:06.595 --> 00:00:09.178
(upbeat music)

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:15.170
- My name is Meg Olmert
and I'm the author of

00:00:15.170 --> 00:00:18.570
"Made For Each Other: the biology
of the human-animal bond."

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:22.720
Our journey with dogs began
about 45,000 years ago

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:25.560
according to the latest
archeological evidence.

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:29.320
It preceded many hundreds
of thousands of years

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:32.700
with a more casual, and
perhaps distant, pragmatic

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:34.680
relationship with wolves as well.

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:39.170
But about 45,000 years ago we
start to see the bones change

00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:43.810
and indications that A, dogs
are certainly living with us.

00:00:43.810 --> 00:00:47.280
And that living with us
appears to have had certain

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:52.271
effects on their biology
and their physiology.

00:00:52.271 --> 00:00:56.121
That is what enabled them to

00:00:56.129 --> 00:00:58.727
become our best friends. 

00:00:58.727 --> 00:01:01.696
- [Narrator] How does
this relationship work?

00:01:01.696 --> 00:01:06.121
- There is a feedback system
that both neurochemically

00:01:06.121 --> 00:01:08.460
and psychologically and behaviorally

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:11.460
that sets up between you and your pet.

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:14.030
And how good you are to your pet

00:01:14.030 --> 00:01:16.758
is often exactly reflected back.

00:01:16.758 --> 00:01:18.300
Although, what I would say

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:20.610
is they tend to be wildly generous.

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:22.280
More generous than we are.

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:25.632
So, that is why, you know,
they'll be wagging their tail,

00:01:25.632 --> 00:01:28.440
you know, thrilled to see
you when you come home

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:29.860
or if it's a cat it's purring.

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:32.530
And you may be very
distracted but they aren't.

00:01:32.530 --> 00:01:34.800
You are the greatest thing they ever saw

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:37.640
if you've developed this relationship.

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:38.890
If you've earned it.

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:42.310
You have a different
relationship with your own pet

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:45.461
versus your, even your
neighbor's pet, because it's,

00:01:45.461 --> 00:01:47.050
that's your closer family.

00:01:47.050 --> 00:01:51.332
It's a different kind of bond
and when they do FMRI studies

00:01:51.357 --> 00:01:56.292
just like a mother will
have certain brain regions

00:01:56.292 --> 00:01:59.455
light up very strongly in the dopamine

00:01:59.455 --> 00:02:02.202
and oxytocin rich areas
when they look at a picture

00:02:02.210 --> 00:02:06.290
of their baby versus just another infant.

00:02:06.290 --> 00:02:09.560
When you look at a picture of
your dog versus another dog,

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:11.050
you see the same thing.

00:02:11.050 --> 00:02:13.869
If you love dogs you're
gonna get this reward

00:02:13.869 --> 00:02:18.023
to a degree whether you are
looking at your dog or not.

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:20.740
Do therapy dogs work?

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:21.650
Well they certainly can.

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:24.010
Dogs can do amazing things.

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:26.300
With our program Warrior Canine Connection

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:28.110
we work with service members

00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:30.720
and veterans with
post-traumatic stress disorder.

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:34.420
We have the patients who say, "This dog

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:36.490
is better than any drug I ever took.

00:02:36.490 --> 00:02:40.040
I didn't sleep for five
years until this dog

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:41.285
slept next to me."

00:02:41.285 --> 00:02:43.582
- [Narrator] What happens to your body

00:02:43.582 --> 00:02:45.280
when you're with your dog?

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:46.430
- [Meg] Your heart rate comes down,

00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:47.870
your blood pressure comes down,

00:02:47.870 --> 00:02:50.630
your heart rate variability
which is the ability

00:02:50.630 --> 00:02:52.200
of the heart to duck and dive

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:55.620
and respond to stress improves.

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:58.934
You release oxytocin, the opioids,

00:02:58.934 --> 00:03:02.052
adrenaline, and serotonin.

00:03:02.052 --> 00:03:05.560
So, all of these great reward chemicals

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:08.630
and anti-stress chemicals can be released

00:03:08.630 --> 00:03:11.130
in both you and the pet.

00:03:11.130 --> 00:03:13.150
- [Narrator] What is oxytocin?

00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:16.250
- Oxytocin is a very ancient chemical.

00:03:16.250 --> 00:03:18.500
It's in all social mammals.

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:22.190
There's variations on it
that you find in birds

00:03:22.190 --> 00:03:24.360
and in turtles, and in worms.

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:29.351
And it most effectively is
known for releasing breast milk

00:03:29.352 --> 00:03:32.200
and creating labor contractions.

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:34.250
That's what it was first known for.

00:03:34.250 --> 00:03:38.270
So in a sense, it is the
quintessential mammalian hormone

00:03:38.270 --> 00:03:42.450
since live birth and production
of breast milk identifies us

00:03:42.450 --> 00:03:44.740
as mammals and mammary glands, et cetera.

00:03:44.740 --> 00:03:49.710
About 25 years ago they discovered
that besides the oxytocin

00:03:49.710 --> 00:03:53.540
receptors and cells in the body
that are producing oxytocin

00:03:53.540 --> 00:03:55.870
in the breast and the
uterus, lo and behold,

00:03:55.870 --> 00:03:59.580
it's produced throughout the
brain and in all the areas

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:01.486
that control behavior and emotion.

00:04:01.486 --> 00:04:03.470
So what was it doing there?

00:04:03.470 --> 00:04:07.547
And what they discovered was
that it was talking to other

00:04:07.547 --> 00:04:11.946
classic brain neurotransmitters
such as serotonin,

00:04:11.955 --> 00:04:16.940
and dopamine and adrenaline,
and the opioid system.

00:04:16.940 --> 00:04:21.673
And it was setting up, by
doing that, this perfect

00:04:21.673 --> 00:04:26.390
mental physiologic state of calm.

00:04:26.411 --> 00:04:29.970
It is the most powerful
drive and it creates the bond

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:30.980
that lasts a lifetime.

00:04:30.980 --> 00:04:32.553
That's how strong it is.

00:04:33.448 --> 00:04:35.948
(happy music)

